# L — LIMITS
1. Every in-scope requirement must have a traceable identifier.
2. Never invent requirement IDs, test case IDs, or defect IDs.
3. Do not assume that the existence of a test case means the requirement has passed.
4. Distinguish design coverage from execution coverage.
5. Identify requirements without test cases.
6. Identify test cases without requirement links.
7. Identify requirements with failed, blocked, or unexecuted tests.
8. Record approved exclusions and their justification.
9. Preserve bidirectional traceability.
10. Do not remove historical execution results when new runs are performed.
11. Identify requirement changes that may impact existing test cases.
12. Use the exact supplied requirement and test case references.

# E — EXECUTION
1. Collect all approved requirements.
2. Collect related test scenarios.
3. Collect detailed test cases.
4. Map each requirement to relevant test scenarios.
5. Map each scenario to detailed test cases.
6. Collect execution results for the applicable release and environment.
7. Map failed test cases to related defects.
8. Identify untested requirements.
9. Identify unlinked test cases.
10. Assess coverage completeness.
11. Identify gaps and recommend corrective actions.
12. Update the RTM when requirements or test cases change.

# A — ASSESSMENT
Evaluate:

- Requirement coverage.
- Test design coverage.
- Test execution coverage.
- Test pass coverage.
- Requirement-to-test traceability.
- Test-to-requirement traceability.
- Defect traceability.
- Uncovered requirements.
- Orphan test cases.
- Failed requirements.
- Blocked requirements.
- Deferred requirements.
- Change impact.

Use these coverage definitions:

Design Coverage:
Requirements having at least one linked test case.

Execution Coverage:
Requirements having at least one executed linked test case.

Pass Coverage:
Requirements satisfying the agreed execution pass rule for the specified test cycle.

A requirement must not be marked Passed merely because one linked test case passed when other mandatory cases remain unexecuted or failed.

